    "code": "LAB ASSIGNMENT: SIMULATING TWO-LEVEL PAGING\n\nObjective\nTo understand how a Memory Management Unit (MMU) translates a 32-bit logical address into a physical address using a two-level paging system.\n\nBackground Theory\nPaging is a memory-management technique in which virtual memory is divided into fixed-size pages and physical memory is divided into frames of the same size. A page table maps virtual pages to physical frames.\n\nFor a 32-bit virtual address and a page size of 4 KB, the address is divided into three parts:\n1. Page Directory Index: 10 bits (bits 22 to 31).\n2. Page Table Index: 10 bits (bits 12 to 21).\n3. Page Offset: 12 bits (bits 0 to 11).\n\nEach page directory contains 1,024 entries. Each second-level page table also contains 1,024 entries. The offset identifies the byte within a 4 KB page.\n\nTask 1: Address Parsing\nThe directory index is extracted by shifting the address right by 22 bits and applying the mask 0x3FF.\nThe table index is extracted by shifting the address right by 12 bits and applying the mask 0x3FF.\nThe offset is extracted by applying the mask 0xFFF.\n\nTask 2: Data Structure Initialization\nThe PageTable structure contains 1,024 integer entries. Every entry is initialized to -1, which indicates that no physical frame has been assigned.\nThe PageDirectory structure contains 1,024 pointers to PageTable objects. Every pointer is initialized to nullptr.\nThe variable next_free_frame keeps track of the next available physical frame. It starts at zero and increases whenever a new frame is assigned.\n\nTask 3: Address Translation\nThe translate function first parses the virtual address.\nIt checks the page directory using the directory index. If the pointer is nullptr, a new page table is created dynamically.\nIt then checks the selected page table entry. If the entry is -1, a new physical frame is assigned and the frame counter is increased.\nThe physical address is calculated using:\nPhysical Address = (Frame Number << 12) | Offset\n\nTask 4: Execution and Analysis\nThe program translates the four given virtual addresses:\n\n1. 0x00000000\n   Directory index = 0, table index = 0, offset = 0.\n   A new page table and a new physical frame are allocated.\n\n2. 0x00000004\n   Directory index = 0, table index = 0, offset = 4.\n   It uses the existing page table entry and physical frame. No new page fault occurs.\n\n3. 0x00400000\n   Directory index = 1, table index = 0, offset = 0.\n   A new page table and a new physical frame are allocated.\n\n4. 0xFFFFF000\n   Directory index = 1023, table index = 1023, offset = 0.\n   A new page table and a new physical frame are allocated.\n\nMemory Analysis\nThree different page directory entries are used, so three second-level page tables are allocated.\n\nEach page table contains 1,024 integers. Assuming an integer occupies 4 bytes:\nMemory per page table = 1,024 x 4 = 4,096 bytes.\n\nMemory used by three page tables = 3 x 4,096 = 12,288 bytes.\n\nThe page directory contains 1,024 pointers. Its size depends on the system architecture:\n- On a 64-bit system: 1,024 x 8 = 8,192 bytes.\n- On a 32-bit system: 1,024 x 4 = 4,096 bytes.\n\nTherefore, the total memory used by the paging structures is:\n- 64-bit system: 12,288 + 8,192 = 20,480 bytes.\n- 32-bit system: 12,288 + 4,096 = 16,384 bytes.\n\nA single-level page table with 1,048,576 entries of 4 bytes each requires 4,194,304 bytes (4 MB).\n\nThe two-level paging system allocates only the second-level page tables that are needed. In this simulation, it uses 12,288 bytes for page tables instead of 4,194,304 bytes for a complete single-level page table.\n\nConclusion\nThe simulation demonstrates how a two-level paging system separates a virtual address into directory index, table index, and offset. It dynamically creates page tables and physical frames only when required. The given addresses require three second-level page tables, which use 12,288 bytes. This is much less memory than the 4 MB required for a complete single-level page table.\n\nCompilation and Execution\ng++ Two_Level_Paging.cpp -o Two_Level_Paging\n./Two_Level_Paging\n"
